name wn-lmf
# A wordnet in WN-LMF (globalwordnet.github.io/schemas, WN-LMF-1.3.dtd). Read for each wordnet that is written in it
# (Open English WordNet, the Open Multilingual Wordnet's lexicons), each recipe naming its own witness.
#   A lexicon is its label. A lexical entry is its lemma's written form. A synset is its source identifier,
#   shared with the highway; its member list and definitions are assertions about that identifier. A sense is the lexicalization
#   [lemma, language, ILI], or, where its synset names no concept the highway holds, the word with its synset.
#   A syntactic behaviour is its frame, as written.
#   ids are the wordnet's keys: how it points at its things. Each resolves to the thing; members, synset, target and
#   senses are such keys. An ili is CILI's key of a concept, read as the concept; a lexfile is read as the lexicographer
#   file the highway lists.
#   What is no thing (a lemma, a relation, a definition) speaks of the nearest thing it is inside.
format xml
# a long wordnet (the Open English WordNet, the larger OMW lexicons) is parsed a part at a time on every core and read as
# the one tree the whole file is: its keys resolve across the file
split LexicalEntry Synset SyntacticBehaviour

key LexicalEntry id
key Sense id
key Synset id
key SyntacticBehaviour id
thing Lexicon label
thing LexicalEntry Lemma/writtenForm
# a sense is the lexicalization [lemma, language, ILI] (recipes/BRIEF.md: "A word in a language is the lexicalization
# [lemma, language, ILI], the language (an ISO 639 highway node) a part of it, never a context column or a mask bit;
# another language is the same ILI with another language part"): the lemma of the entry it is inside, the language its
# lexicon states (Lexicon language, the format page: "language: Please follow BCP-47"; as written, en, which is ISO
# 639-3's Part1 of English, so the node ISO 639 attests), and the ILI of the synset it refers to (type ili ili, below:
# the node the highway's interlingual index holds). Another wordnet's sense of the same concept in another language is
# the same ILI with another language part (Laplace-Engine#20, #32).
thing Sense ^LexicalEntry/Lemma/writtenForm ^Lexicon/language >synset/ili
# a sense whose synset names no concept the highway holds (ili="in", a concept CILI has not numbered yet) is given no
# stand-in identity: it is the word with its synset, and its synset links by collision when CILI states its ILI
thing Sense synset within
thing Synset id
thing SyntacticBehaviour subcategorizationFrame
part members by space
part senses by space
# a member is written as the key of a lexical entry, or (as the Open Multilingual Wordnet writes it) of a sense, which
# stands for the entry it is inside
refer members LexicalEntry Sense within
refer synset Synset
refer SenseRelation.target Sense
refer SynsetRelation.target Synset
refer senses Sense
# a sense's subcat names the syntactic behaviours it takes, by their keys
part subcat by space
refer subcat SyntacticBehaviour
type ili ili
type lexfile lexfile
type language language
content label language writtenForm partOfSpeech relType subcategorizationFrame Definition ILIDefinition Example Pronunciation variety script Tag category Count ref lexicalized
# a file's bookkeeping, read by nothing: who made it, under what licence, at what version, how confident
# n (a sense's place among its entry's senses) and index (an entry's place among its homographs) number what the file
# already holds in order: the order is the path's, and the numbers are not read
omit n index
# Authors and cited resources are content of this source's records. They do not
# create extra witnesses beside the one corpus trunk.
content dc:source
content adjposition
omit dc:* xmlns:* status note confidenceScore version url email license citation logo lexicographer

# What each part a claim is made of means (Laplace-Engine#32): never the attribute's or the element's name. The words
# are WN-LMF's documentation's: the GWA's format page (globalwordnet.github.io/schemas, "Global WordNet Formats"), the
# Global Wordnet RDF Schema it publishes beside the DTD (wn-lemon-1.3.ttl, its rdfs:comment lines), and the W3C
# OntoLex-Lemon model the format page maps WN-LMF onto ("Words are ontolex:LexicalEntry ... Senses are
# ontolex:LexicalSense, they must have a ontolex:reference ... Synsets are ontolex:LexicalConcept"); and, for what
# WN-LMF took over from Princeton WordNet, WordNet's own manual pages.
# partOfSpeech: a value of WN-LMF's part-of-speech list (WN-LMF-1.3.dtd: partOfSpeech (n|v|a|r|s|t|c|p|x|u)), as
# written ([dog, n]); lexfile: a value of the lexicographer files the highway lists ([synset, noun.animal]); ili: the
# concept the synset's ILI names, a value of the ILI road class; relType: a value of WN-LMF's relation list (the DTD's
# relType enumeration: hypernym, antonym, ...), the relation of a SenseRelation or SynsetRelation.
value - partOfSpeech relType
value lexfile lexfile
value ili ili
# adjposition: the DTD's adjposition (a|ip|p), Princeton WordNet's adjective markers, wninput(5WN): "An adjective may be
# annotated with a syntactic marker indicating a limitation on the syntactic position the adjective may have in
# relation to noun that it modifies ... (p) predicate position (a) prenominal (attributive) position (ip) immediately
# postnominal position": a value of that list, as written ([galore, ip])
value - adjposition
# script: wn-lemon-1.3.ttl, wn:script: "The script as a 4 letter ISO 15924 code": a value of ISO 15924's codes
value - script
# a Tag: the format page, "Grammatical Properties": "Grammatical properties such as gender, number, case, and tense can
# be represented with the <Tag> element ... the value is the text of the tag" and "A universal tagging scheme is not
# prescribed": a value of the lexicon's own tagging scheme, said of the lemma's entry or of the form it is in
# ([gatos, plural])
value - Tag
# a Form: OntoLex, "The other form property relates a lexical entry to a non-preferred ("non-lemma") form that realizes
# the given lexical entry" (the format page's RDF example writes a <Form> as ontolex:otherForm). A form is the thing its
# written form names, so what is said inside it (its tags, its pronunciations, its script) is said of the form.
thing Form writtenForm
relation "other form" Form
# a Sense: OntoLex, "The sense property relates a lexical entry to one of its lexical senses" (the format page: "Senses
# are ontolex:LexicalSense"). The sense is the entry with its synset: the lexicalization.
relation "lexical sense" Sense
# a sense's synset: the format page, "Senses are ontolex:LexicalSense, they must have a ontolex:reference"; OntoLex,
# "The reference property relates a lexical sense to an ontological predicate that represents the denotation of the
# corresponding lexical entry". A sense that is the lexicalization holds its synset's ILI, and refers to its synset:
# [sense, reference, synset]. A sense named by its synset holds it as a part, so no claim is made of it again.
relation "reference" synset
# a sense's subcat: the format page, "Syntactic behaviour can also be given as part of the lexicon and referred to with
# the subcat property"; OntoLex, "The syntactic behavior property relates a lexical entry to one of its syntactic
# behaviors as captured by a syntactic frame"
relation "syntactic behaviour" subcat
# a syntactic behaviour's senses: wn-lemon-1.3.ttl, wn:senseSubcat: "Indicates that a syntactic behaviour applies to a
# specific sense"
relation "applies to" senses
# wn-lemon-1.3.ttl, wn:pronunciation: "Indicates the pronunciation of a form" (the format page: "the <Pronunciation>
# element, which gives the IPA text")
relation "pronunciation of a form" Pronunciation
# wn-lemon-1.3.ttl, wn:count: "The count of usages of a sense in some corpus": a number the lexicon states of the sense,
# an observation
relation "count of usages" Count
# wn-lemon-1.3.ttl, wn:definition: "A definition of the synset"; wn:iliDefinition: "The English standard definition of
# the synset in the ILI"; wn:example: "An example usage of a sense or synset"
relation "definition of the synset" Definition
relation "standard definition of the synset in the ILI" ILIDefinition
relation "example usage" Example
# What stays an unresolved obligation, each with its reason:
#   language (of a Lexicon): the format page, "language: Please follow BCP-47": what the lexicon states, recorded where
#     it means something, as the language part of each of its lexicalizations (thing Sense, above), never as a claim on
#     the lexicon's label, which would be a field's name as its relation (Laplace-Engine#20, #32).
#   ref (of a Requires): "the required wordnet should be loaded as well": a dependence between two sources' trunks, the
#     corpus as the witness (Laplace-Engine#28).
#   lexicalized: the DTD's (true|false) "true", which the format's documentation words nowhere (the GWA's schemas#43
#     lists it as "apparently unused"); the Open Multilingual Wordnet writes "false" for a lexical gap (omw-he's README:
#     "GAP for synsets that are not lexicalized in Hebrew"): the absence of a lexicalization [lemma, language, ILI],
#     Laplace-Engine#20.
#   variety (of a Pronunciation): wn-lemon-1.3.ttl, "Indicates the variety of language the pronunciation represents":
#     said of the pronunciation, which is no thing a claim can be made of here (said of the entry it would say
#     [virtuoso, US]); a qualifier of the pronunciation claim (Laplace-Engine#32).
#   category (of a Tag): "a category attribute which indicates the type of grammatical property" (gender, number): said
#     of the tag, the kind its value is of, not of the entry or form (Laplace-Engine#32).

attest Lexicon language
attest Requires ref
holds LexicalEntry Sense Form
# an entry's claims are one record, its senses in the order the file gives them
together LexicalEntry
attest Lemma partOfSpeech script
attest Form script
attest Pronunciation variety .
attest Tag category .
attest Sense synset subcat lexicalized adjposition
attest Count .
relate SenseRelation relType to target
# an ili written "in" is WN-LMF's mark for a concept proposed to CILI and not yet in it: it names no concept, and is said so
attest Synset ili partOfSpeech lexfile lexicalized Definition ILIDefinition
attest Example .
relate SynsetRelation relType to target
attest SyntacticBehaviour senses
